WEBVTT

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.320
<b>Tajimi Mino Ware Museum</b>

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.460
The wind's really gotten cold!

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.730
It'll be time to wear coats soon.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.360
You're got a lot to learn, Hime-chan.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.830
For real.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.070
Yep, Tajimi we could 
still get some summer days.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.580
Really?

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.950
<i>Since I decided to walk all around Tajimi,</i>

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.470
<i>here we are at the Mino Ware Museum.</i>

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.060
<b>Tajimi Mino Ware Museum</b>

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.060
<b>My Mom Really Is Amazing!</b>

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.780
Wow!

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.750
What a view!

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.740
This feels a bit solemn.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.240
Eh, just take it easy, take it easy.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.230
Boo!

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.500
Welcome!

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.470
Touko-senpai, what are you doing here?

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.650
Were you surprised?

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.780
Apparently the curator who was supposed to 
welcome this foreign group suddenly got sick.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.980
so they called my grandfather.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.870
They asked him if he knew a girl 
who could speak English, make tea,

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.050
and knows a lot about pottery.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.250
Naturally, that's your trifecta.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.630
I knew about it, but I kept my mouth shut!

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.250
You look so lovely in that kimono!

00:02:58.990 --> 00:02:59.970
Thank you!

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.240
Hey, I was going to say that!

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.660
Mino ware has a history 
that goes back 1300 years.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.750
It started with Sue ware, which had 
adopted some Baekje techniques.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.030
Then during the Kamakura period,

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.260
with the influence from Seto, 
glazes started to be applied,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.520
which gave birth to the Koseto, 
Kiseto, and Setoguro styles.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.260
Then there was Shino ware,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.790
which was made for Shino Soushin, 
a tea master from Kyoto.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.010
Nanban trading brought about Oribe ware
 in the Azuchi-Momoyama period.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.500
The styles changed quite radically, huh?

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.000
You're right.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.030
And then once we enter the 17th century,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.430
you see the influence of 
Tokugawa Owari in Ofukei ware.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.620
It totally changed again!

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.520
I really like this tea cup.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.680
I'm not surprised, when a living 
national treasure created it.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.720
Rumors say some of them are worth 
hundreds of thousands of yen.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.890
You don't have to be so nervous.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.890
That was so good.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.240
But Mino ware has really gone 
through so many changes.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.990
That's true.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.960
I believe what sets Mino ware
 apart is its openness.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.360
Instead of being limited by tradition,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.790
it has taken in different 
cultures with open arms.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.780
Taking in different cultures, huh?

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.840
It really is quite a thing.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:34.110
The pottery shops of this town have 
taken in a lot of foreign potters.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.960
Wow. I wouldn't have thought people 
from all over the world came here.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:42.290
I bet you thought it was just some 
town out in the sticks

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:44.470
with nothing remarkable except
 its really hot summers.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.980
Huh? No, I never thought that...

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.920
That's the face of someone who thought it.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.820
Looks like you're treating us!

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.480
Seriously?

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.580
Maybe I should order a mille crepe 
on top of my maple marron latte.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.470
I'm in!

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.670
Please go easy on me.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.630
Excuse me!

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.250
May I ask a question?

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.560
S-Sure!

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.650
Where is Soruratorzenoniwa, where is it?

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.610
The Sorurat, Zenoniwa?

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.530
What is she talking about?

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.240
Wait, I got it!

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.840
She means the Zenoni rock!

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.070
The Zenoni rock?

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.550
There's a rock called that, 
somewhere around here.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.710
Wait, moment.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.600
I'm not finding a Zenoni rock...

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.560
Hey all!

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.040
What would Ori Academy's pottery 
club be doing out here?

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.810
Oosawa-san! Perfect timing!

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.800
You work for the city hall,

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.900
so you must know where Sorurat 
or the Zenoni rock are.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:52.810
It must be some famous sight-seeing spot!

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.240
Apparently this person wants to go there.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.340
Sorura or the Zenoni rock?

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.430
I have picture!

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.740
Right here.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.250
Here it is!

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:08.760
This is...

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.740
Hime-chan, it's the sculpture your mom made!

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.960
The Garden of Sky and Wind 
(<i>Sora to Kaze no Niwa</i>)...

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.610
Soratto and Kazenoniiwa.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.900
Oh, I get it now!

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.150
¿Qué? ¿Qué?

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.380
That's Spanish.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.250
Do you understand her?

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.700
I was a Spanish major.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:30.920
Lo siento.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.140
Malentendí un poco.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:39.660
Puedo conducir hasta el 
Jardín del Cielo y el Viento.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.060
Wow! ¡Eso es genial!

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.810
Why does she know about my 
mom's sculpture, though?

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.670
Why my mother?

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.250
Este monumento fue realizado
 por su madre.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:53.450
¡Increíble!

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:54.130
Amazing!

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.110
Wonderful!

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.440
U-Um...

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.800
My name is Ximena!

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.440
Just like my mom?

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.590
¡Qué agradable!

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.310
¡Este es un encuentro milagroso!

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.520
Is it okay if we go with you?

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.470
¡Sí! Of course!

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.560
Then let's stop by my house first.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:17.380
Got it!

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.280
Take care!

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.570
We will!

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:27.370
Ximena-san said she's come all the way 
from Mexico just to see the sculpture.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:32.090
Isn't Mexico on the other 
side of the world?!

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.580
That's dedication!

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:41.110
Está detrás de ese edificio.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:44.390
Tengo mariposas en el estómago.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.640
But how did you hear about the sculpture?

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.770
I was in pottery school in México.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.990
We had teacher from Japón.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.720
Ah, so your teacher was Japanese.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.120
The teacher showed us
 Japón pottery magazine.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.740
It was in there.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.680
Oh, like those things I read!

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.220
As soon as I see picture, that mystery 
form, that wonderful color.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:10.670
¡Excelente!

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:16.190
And the magazine said it will teach 
the voice and color of wind.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.400
My mother said that.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.610
I read that in an article here.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.170
That she made it because she wanted people 
to know the voice and color of the wind.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.190
What a genius comment.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:30.020
Tokikawa Himena really was amazing!

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.250
I want see the real thing! I want to see!

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:36.460
I work hard, saving money 
and now I come to Tajimi!

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.080
¡Brillo del cielo...!

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.220
She said it shines like 
something not of this world.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:30.510
Eee, that northerly wind!

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:43.370
This sound is different from 
the one I heard before.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:45.930
It's such a...

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.160
gentle sound.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:03.290
In winter you get a different sound than 
in summer, with its southerly wind.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.680
It's a soft, kind sound.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.070
¡Excelente!

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.120
¡Himeno, tu madre es la mejor!

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.550
She said, "Your mother is the greatest."

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.990
Ximena-san!

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:26.340
Then experience one more 
thing from my mother.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:34.660
Wow, Himena made this, too?

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.270
So this is why we stopped by your house.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:40.870
Gracias.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:45.940
The wind stopped.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.630
But I see the wind.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:56.840
Thank you for today.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:01.600
Thank you, for coming all this way 
to see something my mother made.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.430
So what are you going to 
do now? Sightseeing?

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.690
¡No, no, esto es todo para mí!

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.450
Camping?

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.180
Up in those hills?

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.920
Won't she get cold at night?

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.660
She brought a sleeping bag.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:23.350
But doesn't that area belong to the city?

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:27.700
Apparently, it's okay because Oosawa-san 
said she'd stay with her for the night.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.200
She came because she wanted 
to see that sculpture, huh.

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.210
Looks like my daughter 
was actually something.

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:38.710
Not just a little Miss Outrageous.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:41.840
A little miss what now?

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.720
That really is amazing, though.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:51.460
Her work drew someone over from 
the other side of the globe.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:02.480
<i>And then, a couple of days 
later, in the evening...</i>

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.060
It's only five, but it's already so dark.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:08.420
The sun sets earlier in the fall.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.190
Himeno!

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.300
Ximena-san! How?

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.590
I've decided to move to Tajimi!

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:18.770
Wow! Really?

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:20.270
Really!

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.980
I'm going to study pottery 
making at Kokeigama!

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:24.050
Wow!

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:25.970
That's some fancy footwork!

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:28.100
See you later, Hime-cha!

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:32.940
Whoa, she's got guts.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:37.450
I feel like she's going to 
make something amazing.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:39.620
What will it be?

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.910
The moon's reflecting!

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.890
Is that something good?

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:47.290
They say that if you stand 
on the Tajimi Bridge

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.370
and see the moon reflected 
in the Toki River,

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.220
your dreams will come true.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.310
Wow, really?

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:58.420
Then I might end up making something 
really amazing to put on that shelf!

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.040
That sounds good!

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:06.370
The breeze is kind of warm today.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:07.790
It is.

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.570
The curly haired one is loud!

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:35.390
<b>Mini Mini Pottery Club</b>

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.850
The curly haired one is random!

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.750
But the curly haired one is the one I...

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.810
Next time, we have
 'The Mumblings of the Clay'.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:40.050
<b>Next Time:</b>

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:40.050
<b>The Mumblings of the Clay</b>

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.300
It's born! It's born!

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.380
What was born?
